(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-pulmonary-pathophysiology-quiz"]={"title":"Pulmonary Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Myasthenia gravis causes acute respiratory failure through which category of cause?","choices":["Neuromuscular disease","Central nervous system","Chest skeletal system","Pulmonary parenchyma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neuromuscular causes are multiple sclerosis, myasthenia gravis, polio, Guillain-Barré syndrome and quadriplegia. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which features describe idiopathic pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Slowly progressive, commoner in men","Results from a known disease process","Usually begins after the seventh decade","Rapidly progressive, commoner in women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic pulmonary hypertension is rapidly progressive, more common in women than men, of unknown cause, and appears in the third to fourth decade. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is a pneumoconiosis?","choices":["Lung disease from inhaled organic agents","Granulomas from an unknown trigger","Infection from aspirated flora","Lung disease from inhaled inorganic dust"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumoconiosis is parenchymal lung disease caused by inhalation of inorganic dust particles such as coal, silica and asbestos. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Where does the airway close in obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Collapsible segments of the pharynx","Small bronchioles","The alveolar ducts","The trachea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea-hypopnea syndrome is complete or partial closure of the collapsible segments of the pharynx during sleep. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What does a low FEV1/FVC (forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity) ratio identify?","choices":["Expiratory airflow limitation","A restrictive pattern","Incomplete emptying","A gas-transfer problem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A low FEV1/FVC (forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity) ratio identifies expiratory airflow limitation, the obstructive pattern. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which condition is associated with an EXUDATIVE pleural effusion?","choices":["Severe heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Nephrotic syndrome","Cirrhosis with ascites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exudates are associated with malignancies, infections, pulmonary embolism, sarcoidosis, post-myocardial infarction syndrome and pancreatic disease. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"On room air, which arterial oxygen tension (PaO2) defines the hypoxemia of acute respiratory failure?","choices":["Below 40 mmHg","Below 50 mmHg","Below 60 mmHg","Below 70 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute respiratory failure is disturbed gas exchange with arterial oxygen below 60 mmHg, carbon dioxide above 50 mmHg or pH below 7.30, breathing room air (21% oxygen). — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why do the lungs in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease fail to deflate normally?","choices":["Air is trapped in damaged air sacs","The pleural space fills with air","Surfactant production rises","The alveolar walls stiffen with fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lungs do not deflate like normal lungs because air is trapped in damaged air sacs, while bronchioles narrow and block with mucus. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 30, 32"},{"q":"What does the term “blue bloater” reflect in chronic bronchitis?","choices":["Alveolar destruction without fibrosis","Air trapped in distended bullae","Reversible bronchospasm with wheeze","Cyanosis and edema of right heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. “Blue bloater” reflects oxygen desaturation (cyanosis) and the edema associated with right-sided heart failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 48, 50"},{"q":"Under which group of restrictive disorders is acute respiratory distress syndrome classified?","choices":["Fibrotic interstitial diseases","Pleural space disorders","Neuromuscular and chest wall disorders","Atelectatic parenchymal disorders"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute (adult) and infant respiratory distress syndromes are the atelectatic disorders of the lung parenchyma. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 67, 68"},{"q":"When does community-acquired pneumonia develop?","choices":["After 4 days in hospital","When surfactant is overproduced","When the pleural space fills with air","When normal defenses are compromised"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal defenses (immune responses, cough reflex, sneezing, mucociliary clearance) protect the lung; community-acquired pneumonia occurs when they are compromised. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 106, 108"},{"q":"What is the characteristic lesion of sarcoidosis?","choices":["Noncaseating epithelioid granulomas","Caseating granulomas with necrosis","Hyaline membranes in the alveoli","Subpleural apical blebs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sarcoidosis develops multiple, uniform, noncaseating epithelioid granulomas, most commonly in the lymph nodes and lung, surrounded by large histiocytes. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why does loss of lung tissue raise airway resistance in emphysema?","choices":["Mucus plugs fill the bronchial lumen","Bronchial smooth muscle contracts","Fibrosis stiffens the airway walls","Radial traction holding airways open is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loss of lung tissue removes the radial traction that holds the airways open, increasing airway resistance and decreasing airflow; trapped air distends air sacs into bullae. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Across how many thin layers of the respiratory membrane do oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse?","choices":["Two","Four","Six","Eight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse across six thin layers: alveolar fluid with surfactant, alveolar epithelium, its basement membrane, the interstitial space, the capillary basement membrane and the capillary endothelium. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why can airflow testing be normal in a person with asthma?","choices":["The obstruction is irreversible","The alveoli are destroyed","Symptoms fluctuate between episodes","Airway inflammation is absent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asthma symptoms fluctuate and testing may normalize between episodes, so the history is read alongside objective airflow measurements. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 35, 44"},{"q":"Which feature separates asthma from the other obstructive airway diseases?","choices":["Its obstruction is persistent","Its obstruction is reversible","It destroys alveolar walls","It dilates the bronchi for good"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is an obstructive airway disease that is reversible, with airway inflammation and increased airway responsiveness to a variety of stimuli. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 35, 48"},{"q":"What does persistent alveolitis lead to in diffuse interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Obliterated capillaries and irreversible fibrosis","Bullae from trapped air","Permanent bronchial dilation","Return of normal alveolar walls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent alveolitis leads to obliteration of alveolar capillaries, reorganization of lung parenchyma and irreversible fibrosis, with large air-filled cysts. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What thickens the walls of small pulmonary vessels in pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Lipid plaques in the intima","Thinning of the media","More muscle mass in the media","Calcium in the adventitia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic exposure thickens the small pulmonary vessel walls through increased muscle mass of the media, driven by local tissue hypoxia and acidosis, and the intima becomes fibrotic. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which change increases net fluid filtration out of a capillary?","choices":["A rise in plasma colloid osmotic pressure","A rise in interstitial fluid pressure","A rise in capillary hydrostatic pressure","A fall in interstitial colloid osmotic pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Net filtration Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pif) − (πc − πif)]: raising capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) pushes more fluid out, as in the effusions of heart failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 17, 93"},{"q":"Through which chain does chronic bronchitis lead to cor pulmonale?","choices":["Left ventricular failure, pulmonary edema, right ventricular atrophy","Embolism, dead space, hypercapnia, left atrial enlargement","Plaque rupture, coronary thrombosis, right ventricular infarction","Hypoxic vasoconstriction, pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular dilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary artery vasoconstriction from hypoxia results in pulmonary hypertension, then right ventricular dilation (cor pulmonale) and right-sided heart failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does pulmonary embolism push toward hypercapnic ventilatory failure?","choices":["It increases carbon dioxide production","It blunts sensing of carbon dioxide","It increases dead space","It blocks nerve signals to the muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased dead space (wasted ventilation), from hypovolemia, low cardiac output or pulmonary embolism, leaves ventilation inadequate for the carbon dioxide produced. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 10, 14"},{"q":"Which alveolar cell secretes surfactant?","choices":["Type I pneumocyte","Alveolar macrophage","Capillary endothelial cell","Type II pneumocyte"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The type II pneumocyte is the surfactant-secreting cell of the alveolar wall; type I pneumocytes form most of the thin alveolar epithelium. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What defines the restrictive lung diseases?","choices":["Decreased expansion of the lungs","Increased airflow resistance","Reversible airway inflammation","Dilation of the bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive lung diseases are manifested by decreased expansion of the lungs due to alterations in the parenchyma, pleura, chest wall or neuromuscular function. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Who is the typical patient with a primary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["An older adult with emphysema","A tall, thin man aged 20–40","A trauma patient with a chest wound","A child with cystic fibrosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in tall, thin men aged 20–40 with no underlying disease; cigarette smoking increases the risk. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A pleural fluid meets only the lactate dehydrogenase ratio criterion for an exudate. How is it classified?","choices":["Transudate","Exudate","Indeterminate until two criteria are met","Chylothorax"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. One positive criterion supports exudate classification; a pleural to serum lactate dehydrogenase ratio above 0.6 is an exudate criterion even when the protein ratio is below 0.5. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 94, 95, 98"},{"q":"What distinguishes hypoxia from hypoxemia?","choices":["Hypoxia is low tissue oxygenation","Hypoxia is low arterial oxygen","Hypoxia is high arterial carbon dioxide","Hypoxia is low hemoglobin saturation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxia is decreased tissue oxygenation; hypoxemia is decreased blood oxygen, seen as a low arterial oxygen and low hemoglobin saturation. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What ruptures in a primary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["A bronchus from penetrating trauma","Small subpleural blebs at the apices","A cyst from preexisting lung disease","The thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary pneumothorax results from rupture of small subpleural blebs in the apices: air enters the pleural space, the lung collapses and the rib cage springs out. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"Why is squamous cell carcinoma of the lung often detected earlier than other lung cancers?","choices":["It doubles in about 33 days","Sputum cytology can show it","It arises in the lung periphery","Its large cells form visible nests"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two-thirds arise in the central bronchi near the hilus as intraluminal growth, so sputum cytology shows the cancer and detection comes earlier. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 25, 26, 27"},{"q":"What produces the purulent, foul-smelling sputum of bronchiectasis?","choices":["Mast cells release leukotrienes","Immune complexes fill the alveoli","Dilated airways act as infection pockets","Chyle leaks into the pleura"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchiolar dilatations serve as pockets of infection, producing purulent, foul-smelling sputum; Haemophilus influenzae is the most common bacterial cause. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What injury starts acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Rupture of subpleural blebs","Damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane","Loss of radial traction on airways","Closure of the collapsible pharynx"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute respiratory distress syndrome begins with damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane, giving widespread alveolar infiltrates and severe dyspnea. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 84"}]};
